{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "An Overview on C#", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 1, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 3, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear and use when talking about C#. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe a general-purpose computer programming language, regarding its history and architecture in spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le03ob01re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Future With Will \u2013 Interrogative Form", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "The auxiliary <strong>will</strong> helps us talk about the future. When making questions, <strong>will</strong> is in a specific position within the sentence.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You ask questions with <strong>will</strong> when you request information about a future fact.  As usual, you can ask for specific information making an open question or you can also ask yes/no questions. This is the structure that you follow to create these questions: </br></br>Closed Questions:  </br></br>  <strong>Will</strong> + subject + verb + complement + ?  </br></br>  Example:<i> <strong>Will</strong> you program the application with C++ or C#?</i> </br></br>Open Questions:  </br></br>  Wh question word + <strong>will</strong> + subject + verb + complement + ?  </br></br>  Example: <i>When <strong>will</strong> you issue the new application?</i>  </br></br>  Sometimes, you can find a question with <strong>will</strong> in which you don't necessarily ask for information, but in a <strong>very informal</strong> way, you request someone to do something.</br></br>  Example: <i><strong>Will</strong> you hand me the laptop, please?</i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Will programmers be able to create websites with that language?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Will C# still be so popular in the following years?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Will you give a course on C# soon?", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "How + Adjective", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We use this <strong>how + adjective</strong> structure to ask about the degree of quality of an object.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Form: <strong>How + adjective</strong> + verb to be + pronoun + infinitive?  </br> </br> Example: <i><strong>How necessary</strong> is it to learn C language first? </i>  </br></br>  Form: <strong>How + adjective</strong> + verb to be + object </br></br>Example: <i><strong>How important</strong> is C language?</i>   </br></br>  Pronunciation: It is an information question, so it has a falling intonation; it falls at the end.  </br></br>  If you invert the order how + adjective + subject + verb, it becomes an exclamation:   </br></br>  Example: Now I know <strong>how awesome</strong> C language is!", "type": "text"}, {"content": "How useful is C# language?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "How good is C# language for you on a scale of 0-10?", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "runtime", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the length of time a program last to run. ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It also consists of a runtime environment that configures the device to run code produced by the compiler.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le03ob01aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "general-purpose ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "that can be used towards reaching several objectives", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It is a general-purpose programming language; you can write almost any program or app with it.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le03ob01aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "of choice", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the most widely preferred option", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(idiom)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: C# is the coding language of choice for operating system development.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le03ob01aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "a number of ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a group of several things", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(idiom)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It also has some important programming features, including conditional compilation, assembly versioning, automatic garbage collection, standard library, and integration with Windows, among a number of other features.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le03ob01aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "still", "type": "name"}, {"content": "keeping the same conditions up to certain time ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adverb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Will C# still be so popular in the following years?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le03ob01aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "expertise ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "advanced knowledge on a specific subject", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Y