": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 12, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n5le03ob01re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<strong>Millie: </strong></strong> How useful is C# language? </br>\n<strong>Mark: </strong> It is very useful. It is a general-purpose programming language; you can write almost any program or app with it. </br>\n<strong>Millie: </strong> Will programmers be able to create websites with that language? </br>\n<strong>Mark: </strong> No, it's not possible. But C# is the coding language of choice for operating system development, and as you might know, the operating system controls every other program on a computer. Nowadays, C# is used to write codes of all levels thanks to being a small, efficient, powerful, and fast executable code. It also consists of a runtime environment that configures the device to run code produced by the compiler. </br>\n<strong>Millie: </strong> Well, now I know it's very useful, but how popular is it?", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob01re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "52000", "text": "<strong>Mark: </strong> C# has several characteristics that make it popular amongst programmers. It is object oriented and component oriented, you can compile it on several computer platforms, and the best part is that it is really easy to learn. It also has some important programming features, including conditional compilation, assembly versioning, automatic garbage collection, standard library, and integration with Windows, among a number of other features.", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob01re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "86000", "text": "<strong>Millie: </strong> Will C# still be so popular in the following years? </br>\n<strong>Mark: </strong> We don't really know, but it is very likely. </br>\n<strong>Millie: </strong> How good is C# language for you on a scale of 0-10? </br>\n<strong>Mark: </strong> For me is a 10. It's a low-level language, which means it's closer to the machines. It is a tight language, which means that each variable must have a declared type. </br>\n<strong>Millie: </strong> Your expertise relating to this language is amazing; will you give a course on C# soon? </br>\n<strong>Mark: </strong> I don't think so. I'm good at explaining things, but then when it comes to speaking in public, I get terrified.", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob01re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le03ob01re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}